Q: Type &#39;null&#39; is not assignable to type &#39;NullabilityOf&lt;T, PhotoHelper&gt;&#39; A: <p>You can cast the return value to <code>NullabilityOf&lt;T, PhotoHelper&gt;</code> to avoid <code>any</code>.  You also might want to actually return undefined in the factory method, since <code>PhotoHelper.from(undefined)</code> currently returns <code>null</code> typed as  <code>PhotoHelper | undefined</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>static from&lt;T extends Photo | null | undefined&gt;(
    image: T
): NullabilityOf&lt;T, PhotoHelper&gt; {
    let photoHelper: null | undefined | PhotoHelper;
    if (image === null) photoHelper = null;
    if (image != undefined) photoHelper = new PhotoHelper(image);
    return photoHelper as NullabilityOf&lt;T, PhotoHelper&gt;;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I stop my overlay from overflowing the site body? A: <p>The main reason your overlay is causing an overflow (and why your &quot;hidden&quot; fix is breaking things) is a conflict in your inline CSS.</p>
<p>In your <code>div</code> style, you have declared the <code>position</code> property three times</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div style=&quot;... position: fixed; ... position: absolute; position:relative; ...&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>CSS rules apply from left to right, and the last one wins. So your element is currently <code>position: relative</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Relative positioning keeps the element in the &quot;flow&quot; of the page.</li>
<li>When you apply <code>right: -200px</code>, it physically widens the document to accommodate that extra space, creating a scrollbar.</li>
</ul>
<p>Just remove the conflicting positions. If you want the teeth to stay stuck to the screen corner while you scroll, use <code>fixed</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div style=&quot;position: fixed; right: -200px; bottom: -200px; z-index: 20; pointer-events: none;&quot;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;teeth1&quot;&gt;
        &lt;img src=&quot;teeth.png&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>You about using <code>body { overflow-x: hidden; }</code> cuts off the &quot;opposite half&quot; of your site. This is because of your layout CSS, not the overlay.</p>
<p>Your header has this CSS</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.header {
    width: 800px;
    margin: 0 20%;
    /* ... */
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>The Math: If your screen is 1000px wide...
<ul>
<li>20% left margin = <code>200px</code></li>
<li>Width = <code>800px</code></li>
<li>20% right margin = <code>200px</code></li>
<li>Total Required Width = <code>1200px</code></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Your content is 1200px wide on a 1000px screen.</p>
<p>Normally, the browser lets you scroll to see the rest. But when you add <code>overflow-x: hidden</code>, the browser clips the excess. Since the content is centered, it clips the left and right edges, making it look like the &quot;opposite half&quot; is missing.</p>
